---
Part XIII, Chapter 4: The boundaries of a trope
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The boundaries of a trope

A trope gathers the chapters that serve one purpose, such as the payments of a shop. A program keeps such a cluster whole by drawing a boundary around it. A few chapters are the ways in, which other code may use, and the rest is the inside, which nobody outside touches. A package's `exports` field draws that line for npm, and a module's `exports` clause draws it for Java. A trope draws it in the frontmatter of its chapters.

```
---
Part III, Trope Chapter 4: Payments
Entries: Charging, Refunds
---
```

```
---
Part III, Chapter 5: Charging
Rooted in: Payments
---
```

The trope chapter roots the trope and gives it its title for a name, as the chapter "The kinds tropes declare" describes. A `Rooted in:` line makes a chapter one of the trope's members. The root's `Entries:` line lists the trope's entries, the ways in: the chapters that a chapter outside the trope may reach. The root is an entry only when it lists itself, as a package's `package.json` declares the package without being a way in. A root with no `Entries:` line is the trope's only entry, as the root of the Mermaid trope in Part XIV is.

## Tropes inside tropes

A trope chapter may itself be `Rooted in:` another trope. Its trope then nests inside the other, as a module nests inside a package, and the inner trope's chapters belong to the outer trope too.

```
---
Part III, Trope Chapter 6: Ledger
Rooted in: Payments
---
```

Loom checks every reach between two chapters by one rule. A chapter may reach another when, for every trope that holds the other chapter but not the first, the other chapter is one of that trope's entries. Put plainly, a chapter sees all of every trope it stands in, and only the entries of the tropes it stands outside.

So the chapters of an inner trope see the chapters of the outer trope, and the outer trope sees only the entries of the inner one. A chapter outside both needs an entry at each boundary it crosses. An outer trope shows a chapter of an inner trope to the world by listing it among its own entries, and it may do so only when the chapter is an entry of the inner trope as well.

Loom never asks an author to list a trope's inside. Every chapter of a trope that is not an entry lies inside it. A chapter reaches another when an anchor in it names the other, or when a box in it points to the other, as the chapter "The multi-file AST" lists.

## What Loom judges

Loom judges a trope's boundary in these places:

- A reach that crosses a boundary to a chapter that is no entry there is an error, at the anchor or the box that makes it.
- An entry that names no chapter, a chapter of an unrelated trope, or a chapter private to a trope nested inside is an error, on the name.
- A `Rooted in:` line that names no trope chapter is a note: the trope has no root yet, so Loom draws no boundary around the chapter.
- Tropes rooted in each other in a loop are an error.
- An `Entries:` line on a chapter that is no trope chapter is an error.
- A chapter that names no trope, but that a trope's chapter reaches, is a note: its relation to the trope is undeclared. Such a chapter sees inside the trope that reaches it. It stays open to every other chapter, since nothing declares it private.

## A trope's boundary

A `TropeNode` holds what Loom derives for one trope with a root: its `name`, its `root`, the `parent` trope its root is rooted in, when it is, the names its `Entries:` line lists with the chapters they lead to, and the paths of the `entries` themselves. A `Listed` pairs one such name with the chapter it leads to, when it leads to one.

`Boundaries` holds a node for each trope with a root, by name, beside two maps. `chains` gives each chapter of such a trope the tropes that hold it, innermost first. `derived` gives each chapter that names no trope the trope whose chapter reaches it first.

=>

export type Listed = {
  readonly name: FrontmatterEntryToken
  readonly chapter: Option.Option<LoomModule>
}

export type TropeNode = {
  readonly name: string
  readonly root: LoomModule
  readonly parent: Option.Option<string>
  readonly listed: ReadonlyArray<Listed>
  readonly entries: ReadonlyArray<Location>
}

export type Boundaries = {
  readonly tropes: ReadonlyMap<string, TropeNode>
  readonly chains: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<string>>
  readonly derived: ReadonlyMap<Location, string>
}

## Reading the tropes

`nodeOf` builds a trope's node from its root, and `parentOf` reads the trope a root is rooted in. Each name on the root's `Entries:` line leads to a chapter through the corpus's own `tropeEntryModuleOf`, which resolves the name as an anchor's name resolves in the root's loom. A root with no `Entries:` line has itself for its only entry.

`chainOf` walks from a trope to the tropes it nests in, through each parent in turn. It stops at a trope it has already passed, so tropes rooted in each other in a loop give a chain that ends rather than one that runs forever.

=>

const parentOf = (root: LoomModule): Option.Option<string> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(root.doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn), (line) => line.value)

const nodeOf = (catalogue: Catalogue, trope: Trope, root: LoomModule): TropeNode => {
  const names = Option.fromNullishOr(root.doc.frontmatter?.entries)
  const listed = Array.map(
    Option.getOrElse(names, () => []),
    (name): Listed => ({ name, chapter: tropeEntryModuleOf(catalogue, root.path, name.value) }),
  )
  return {
    name: trope.name,
    root,
    parent: parentOf(root),
    listed,
    entries: Option.isNone(names)
      ? [root.path]
      : Array.getSomes(Array.map(listed, (entry) => Option.map(entry.chapter, (m) => m.path))),
  }
}

const chainOf = (
  tropes: ReadonlyMap<string, TropeNode>,
  name: string,
  seen: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set(),
): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  seen.has(name)
    ? []
    : Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(tropes.get(name)), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
        onSome: (node) => [
          name,
          ...Option.match(node.parent, {
            onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
            onSome: (parent) => chainOf(tropes, parent, new Set([...seen, name])),
          }),
        ],
      })

## Gathering the boundaries

`boundariesOf` derives the boundaries of a corpus from the tropes the registry gathered. It builds a node for each trope with a root, and gives each chapter of such a trope its chain. A chapter whose trope has no root gets no chain, so no boundary holds it.

It then finds the chapters that name no trope but that a trope's chapter reaches, along the reaches that `reachesOf` lists. Each one takes, as its derived trope, the trope of the first chapter that reaches it, in the order of the chapters' paths. A discussion belongs to the book rather than to a trope, so `namesNoTrope` leaves discussions out.

=>

const namesNoTrope = (module: LoomModule): boolean =>
  Option.isNone(tropeOf(module)) && module.doc.frontmatter?.discussion === undefined

export const boundariesOf = (
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  gathered: ReadonlyArray<Trope>,
): Boundaries => {
  const tropes: ReadonlyMap<string, TropeNode> = new Map(
    Array.getSomes(
      Array.map(gathered, (trope) =>
        Option.map(trope.root, (root) => [trope.name, nodeOf(catalogue, trope, root)] as const),
      ),
    ),
  )
  const held = pipe(
    gathered,
    Array.filter((trope) => tropes.has(trope.name)),
    Array.flatMap((trope) => trope.chapters),
    Array.sort(Order.mapInput(Order.String, (module: LoomModule) => module.path)),
  )
  const chains = new Map(
    Array.map(held, (module) => [
      module.path,
      Option.match(tropeOf(module), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
        onSome: (name) => chainOf(tropes, name),
      }),
    ]),
  )
  const derived = Array.reduce(held, new Map<Location, string>(), (found, module) =>
    Option.match(tropeOf(module), {
      onNone: () => found,
      onSome: (trope) =>
        Array.reduce(reachesOf(catalogue, module), found, (was, each) =>
          was.has(each.target) ||
          !Option.exists(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(each.target)), namesNoTrope)
            ? was
            : new Map(was).set(each.target, trope),
        ),
    }),
  )
  return { tropes, chains, derived }
}

## Judging a loom against the boundaries

`boundaryFaults` gives a loom the faults its tropes' boundaries find in it, one kind at a time.

`entriesOffRoot` faults an `Entries:` line on a chapter that is no trope chapter, at the line's first name. `rootedInFaults` judges a `Rooted in:` line. One that names no trope chapter is the `UnrootedTrope` note. The root of a trope that nests in itself, through any number of tropes, carries the `CircularRoots` fault, with the loop that `loopOf` reads. `undeclaredFaults` gives a chapter of a derived trope the `UnsettledMember` note on its title, which `titleLineOf` finds, since the frontmatter is where the chapter would name the trope.

=>

const titleLineOf = (module: LoomModule): Position =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.title), (title) => title.position),
    () => ({ start: module.doc.position.start, end: module.doc.position.start }),
  )

const entriesOffRoot = (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  rootsTrope(module)
    ? []
    : pipe(
        Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.entries),
        Option.flatMap(Array.head),
        Option.match({
          onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
          onSome: (name) => faulty(EntriesOffRoot(), name.position).diagnostics,
        }),
      )

const loopOf = (
  tropes: ReadonlyMap<string, TropeNode>,
  start: string,
): Option.Option<ReadonlyArray<string>> => {
  const walk = (name: string, path: ReadonlyArray<string>): Option.Option<ReadonlyArray<string>> =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(tropes.get(name)),
      Option.flatMap((node) => node.parent),
      Option.flatMap((parent) =>
        Match.value(parent).pipe(
          Match.when(
            (next: string) => next === start,
            (next) => Option.some([...path, next]),
          ),
          Match.when(
            (next: string) => path.includes(next),
            () => Option.none<ReadonlyArray<string>>(),
          ),
          Match.orElse((next) => walk(next, [...path, next])),
        ),
      ),
    )
  return walk(start, [start])
}

const rootedInFaults = (boundaries: Boundaries, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter?.rootedIn), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
    onSome: (line) =>
      !boundaries.tropes.has(line.value)
        ? faulty(UnrootedTrope({ trope: line.value }), line.position).diagnostics
        : Option.match(
            Option.flatMap(
              Option.filter(tropeOf(module), () => rootsTrope(module)),
              (own) => loopOf(boundaries.tropes, own),
            ),
            {
              onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
              onSome: (loop) => faulty(CircularRoots({ loop }), line.position).diagnostics,
            },
          ),
  })

const undeclaredFaults = (boundaries: Boundaries, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.derived.get(module.path)), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
    onSome: (trope) => faulty(UnsettledMember({ trope }), titleLineOf(module)).diagnostics,
  })

~

`entryFaults` judges each name a root's `Entries:` line lists, and `listedFault` judges one. A name that leads to no chapter is the `UnknownEntry` fault. A chapter held by tropes that leave the root's trope out belongs to another trope, and is the `ForeignEntry` fault. A chapter of an inner trope must be an entry of every trope it stands in below the root's own, and the first trope it is not an entry of makes it the `PrivateEntry` fault. A chapter that names no trope may stand as an entry, and Loom notes its undeclared relation instead.

`isEntry` asks whether a chapter is one of a trope's entries, and `chainIn` gives a chapter's chain, or no trope at all.

=>

const isEntry = (boundaries: Boundaries, trope: string, path: Location): boolean =>
  Option.exists(Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.tropes.get(trope)), (node) =>
    node.entries.includes(path),
  )

const chainIn = (boundaries: Boundaries, path: Location): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.chains.get(path)), () => [])

const listedFault = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  trope: string,
  entry: Listed,
): Option.Option<LoomFault> =>
  Option.match(entry.chapter, {
    onNone: () => Option.some(UnknownEntry({ name: entry.name.value })),
    onSome: (chapter) => {
      const chain = chainIn(boundaries, chapter.path)
      const within = chain.indexOf(trope)
      return Option.flatMap(Array.head(chain), (innermost): Option.Option<LoomFault> =>
        within < 0
          ? Option.some(ForeignEntry({ name: entry.name.value, trope: innermost }))
          : Option.map(
              Array.findFirst(
                Array.take(chain, within),
                (inner) => !isEntry(boundaries, inner, chapter.path),
              ),
              (inner) => PrivateEntry({ name: entry.name.value, trope: inner }),
            ),
      )
    },
  })

const entryFaults = (boundaries: Boundaries, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  pipe(
    Array.fromIterable(boundaries.tropes.values()),
    Array.filter((node) => node.root.path === module.path),
    Array.flatMap((node) =>
      Array.flatMap(node.listed, (entry) =>
        Option.match(listedFault(boundaries, node.name, entry), {
          onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
          onSome: (fault) => faulty(fault, entry.name.position).diagnostics,
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )

~

`reachingInside` judges each reach the loom makes. The loom's own chain is the chain it declares, or the chain of its derived trope, since a chapter a trope reaches sees inside that trope. The target's chain is only the chain it declares, since nothing makes an undeclared chapter private. Every trope in the target's chain and not in the loom's is a boundary the reach crosses. `crossedFault` finds the outermost of them where the target is no entry, since that boundary is the one the author must go around, and names the entries the trope offers there instead. Loom lists the loom's reaches only when the corpus holds a trope with a root.

=>

const crossedFault = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  own: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  target: LoomModule,
): Option.Option<LoomFault> =>
  pipe(
    Array.findLast(
      Array.filter(chainIn(boundaries, target.path), (trope) => !own.includes(trope)),
      (trope) => !isEntry(boundaries, trope, target.path),
    ),
    Option.flatMap((trope) => Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.tropes.get(trope))),
    Option.map((node) =>
      ReachingInside({
        chapter: titleOf(target),
        trope: node.name,
        entries: Array.getSomes(
          Array.map(node.entries, (path) =>
            Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), titleOf),
          ),
        ),
      }),
    ),
  )

const reachingInside = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => {
  const own = Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.chains.get(module.path)), {
    onSome: (chain) => chain,
    onNone: () =>
      Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(boundaries.derived.get(module.path)), {
        onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<string> => [],
        onSome: (trope) => chainOf(boundaries.tropes, trope),
      }),
  })
  return boundaries.tropes.size === 0
    ? []
    : Array.flatMap(reachesOf(catalogue, module), (each) =>
        Option.match(
          Option.flatMap(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(each.target)), (target) =>
            crossedFault(boundaries, modules, own, target),
          ),
          {
            onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
            onSome: (fault) => faulty(fault, each.at).diagnostics,
          },
        ),
      )
}

export const boundaryFaults = (
  boundaries: Boundaries,
  catalogue: Catalogue,
  modules: ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>,
  module: LoomModule,
): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
  ...entriesOffRoot(module),
  ...rootedInFaults(boundaries, module),
  ...entryFaults(boundaries, module),
  ...undeclaredFaults(boundaries, module),
  ...reachingInside(boundaries, catalogue, modules, module),
]

## What the boundaries draw on

From `effect` come the array, option, and order combinators, `Match` for the walk along a loop of roots, and `pipe`. From the Loom AST come the `Diagnostic` and `Position` a fault lands with, and the `FrontmatterEntryToken` each name of an `Entries:` line is. The corpus lends the `Catalogue`, the modules, `tropeEntryModuleOf`, which resolves an entry's name, and `reachesOf`, which lists a loom's reaches. The fault catalog lends `faulty` and the faults of a boundary, each a `LoomFault`. The registry of the chapter "The kinds tropes declare" lends the `Trope` it gathers, `tropeOf`, which reads the trope a chapter roots or belongs to, `rootsTrope`, which tells a trope chapter, and `titleOf`, which reads a chapter's title.

=>

import { Array, Match, Option, Order, pipe } from 'effect'
import type { Diagnostic, Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import type { FrontmatterEntryToken } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'
import {
  type Catalogue,
  type Location,
  type LoomModule,
  reachesOf,
  tropeEntryModuleOf,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import {
  CircularRoots,
  EntriesOffRoot,
  faulty,
  ForeignEntry,
  type LoomFault,
  PrivateEntry,
  ReachingInside,
  UnknownEntry,
  UnrootedTrope,
  UnsettledMember,
} from './LoomFault'
import { rootsTrope, titleOf, type Trope, tropeOf } from './DeclaredKinds'

## The module [src/ast/TropeBoundaries.ts]

=>

